1
00:00:00,328 --> 00:00:01,974
What is gradient descent?

2
00:00:02,175 --> 00:00:04,346
It is the algorithm that lets a machine learn:

3
00:00:04,486 --> 00:00:06,689
measure the error, find the direction in which

4
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:09,301
it falls fastest, take a step, and repeat.

5
00:00:10,203 --> 00:00:13,043
It trains almost every neural network in use today,

6
00:00:13,172 --> 00:00:15,777
from image recognition to large language models.

7
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:18,812
But it began long before computers, with a problem

8
00:00:18,673 --> 00:00:19,783
about the planets.

9
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:22,727
Paris, eighteen forty-seven.

10
00:00:22,895 --> 00:00:25,768
Augustin-Louis Cauchy wants to compute the orbit

11
00:00:25,630 --> 00:00:26,985
of a body in the heavens.

12
00:00:27,188 --> 00:00:28,622
He has the equations.

13
00:00:28,806 --> 00:00:31,200
But nothing he knows will solve them exactly.

14
00:00:32,014 --> 00:00:33,660
So he changes the question.

15
00:00:33,831 --> 00:00:36,336
Take any guess, and measure how wrong it is.

16
00:00:36,518 --> 00:00:38,163
One number: the error.

17
00:00:38,965 --> 00:00:41,526
Picture that error for every possible guess.

18
00:00:41,724 --> 00:00:43,168
It forms a landscape.

19
00:00:43,376 --> 00:00:46,037
High where the guess is poor, low where it is good.

20
00:00:46,244 --> 00:00:48,436
The solution sits at the very bottom.

21
00:00:49,299 --> 00:00:51,614
But Cauchy cannot see this landscape.

22
00:00:51,789 --> 00:00:54,808
Every single point costs him pages of arithmetic.

23
00:00:54,992 --> 00:00:58,011
All he can find is the slope, right where he stands.

24
00:00:58,912 --> 00:01:00,624
That slope is the gradient.

25
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:03,130
It points where the error grows fastest.

26
00:01:03,315 --> 00:01:06,076
So he steps the opposite way, and asks again.

27
00:01:06,942 --> 00:01:09,771
From above, the landscape becomes a map of contour

28
00:01:09,632 --> 00:01:10,306
lines.

29
00:01:10,486 --> 00:01:13,582
Every step crosses them at right angles: the steepest

30
00:01:13,443 --> 00:01:14,185
way down.

31
00:01:14,381 --> 00:01:17,131
Step by step, the guess slides into the valley.

32
00:01:17,924 --> 00:01:20,138
Cauchy wrote this up in three pages.

33
00:01:20,305 --> 00:01:22,643
Today it is called gradient descent.

34
00:01:24,056 --> 00:01:25,489
Let's do it by hand.

35
00:01:25,691 --> 00:01:28,408
Here is a landscape we can write down: f of x,

36
00:01:28,437 --> 00:01:31,265
y, equals x squared plus three y squared.

37
00:01:31,461 --> 00:01:34,234
From above, its contours are ellipses, and the

38
00:01:34,095 --> 00:01:35,741
lowest point is at the origin.

39
00:01:36,578 --> 00:01:40,858
Start at x equals -2.6, y equals 1.4.

40
00:01:41,059 --> 00:01:43,296
The error there is 12.64.

41
00:01:44,109 --> 00:01:45,163
Now the slope.

42
00:01:45,364 --> 00:01:48,260
Differentiate with respect to x, treating y as

43
00:01:48,121 --> 00:01:49,823
a constant: two x.

44
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:52,345
Then with respect to y: six y.

45
00:01:52,541 --> 00:01:55,715
Those two numbers, side by side, are the gradient.

46
00:01:56,536 --> 00:02:00,291
At our point, that is -5.2, and 8.4.

47
00:02:00,493 --> 00:02:03,143
The error rises fastest up and to the left.

48
00:02:03,339 --> 00:02:04,750
So we go the other way.

49
00:02:05,567 --> 00:02:06,331
How far?

50
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:09,729
Multiply the gradient by a small number, eta, the

51
00:02:09,591 --> 00:02:11,325
learning rate, and subtract.

52
00:02:11,521 --> 00:02:16,359
With eta equal to 0.12, the new x is -1.98, and

53
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,212
the new y is 0.39.

54
00:02:19,078 --> 00:02:22,599
One step, and the error has fallen from 12.64 to

55
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:23,726
4.37.

56
00:02:23,928 --> 00:02:25,763
That is the entire algorithm.

57
00:02:25,948 --> 00:02:27,805
Everything else is repetition.

58
00:02:29,257 --> 00:02:31,572
But that small number matters enormously.

59
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:34,018
Here are four runs on the same landscape.

60
00:02:34,225 --> 00:02:38,438
With eta at 0.03, the steps are tiny, and it crawls.

61
00:02:39,578 --> 00:02:42,485
At 0.12, it glides into the valley.

62
00:02:43,328 --> 00:02:47,183
At 0.31, it overshoots the steep walls and zig-zags

63
00:02:47,045 --> 00:02:48,199
from side to side.

64
00:02:48,401 --> 00:02:52,245
And at 0.35, every step lands higher than the last.

65
00:02:52,430 --> 00:02:53,785
It never comes back.

66
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:56,131
The reason is exact.

67
00:02:56,322 --> 00:02:59,117
Along the steep direction, each step multiplies

68
00:02:58,978 --> 00:03:00,959
the error by one minus six eta.

69
00:03:01,136 --> 00:03:04,244
Keep that between minus one and one, and you converge.

70
00:03:04,436 --> 00:03:07,733
So eta must stay below one third: two, divided

71
00:03:07,594 --> 00:03:08,994
by the steepest curvature.

72
00:03:09,201 --> 00:03:11,729
The steepest direction sets the speed limit for

73
00:03:11,590 --> 00:03:12,622
everything else.

74
00:03:14,067 --> 00:03:16,036
Stanford, nineteen sixty.

75
00:03:16,216 --> 00:03:19,412
Bernard Widrow and his student, Ted Hoff, are building

76
00:03:19,274 --> 00:03:20,540
a machine that learns.

77
00:03:20,725 --> 00:03:22,058
They call it Adaline.

78
00:03:22,911 --> 00:03:24,412
Their problem is cost.

79
00:03:24,614 --> 00:03:27,197
The true gradient needs the error averaged over

80
00:03:27,058 --> 00:03:27,956
all the data.

81
00:03:28,152 --> 00:03:30,813
So they make a bold simplification: estimate the

82
00:03:30,674 --> 00:03:33,536
gradient from a single example, take a step, and

83
00:03:33,397 --> 00:03:34,094
move on.

84
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,692
Each estimate is noisy, and the path wanders.

85
00:03:37,865 --> 00:03:40,336
But on average it still heads downhill, and it

86
00:03:40,197 --> 00:03:41,530
costs almost nothing.

87
00:03:42,411 --> 00:03:44,280
They called it least mean squares.

88
00:03:44,453 --> 00:03:47,315
Today we call the idea stochastic gradient descent,

89
00:03:47,444 --> 00:03:49,602
and it is how every large model is trained.

90
00:03:51,097 --> 00:03:52,1000
And it went straight into industry.

91
00:03:53,202 --> 00:03:54,780
Here is the classic case.

92
00:03:54,976 --> 00:03:57,693
A microphone picks up a signal we want, buried

93
00:03:57,554 --> 00:04:00,048
under noise that is louder than the signal itself.

94
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:03,263
A second microphone hears only the noise.

95
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:06,416
An adaptive filter, with just two weights, predicts

96
00:04:06,277 --> 00:04:08,593
how that noise leaks into the first microphone,

97
00:04:08,621 --> 00:04:09,709
and subtracts it.

98
00:04:10,537 --> 00:04:12,974
Its error surface is a bowl, and the bottom of

99
00:04:12,836 --> 00:04:14,370
the bowl is the true leak.

100
00:04:14,571 --> 00:04:17,500
After every single sample, the filter takes one

101
00:04:17,362 --> 00:04:18,482
Widrow-Hoff step.

102
00:04:19,328 --> 00:04:21,666
Watch the weights walk downhill, and the signal

103
00:04:21,527 --> 00:04:22,201
emerge.

104
00:04:22,403 --> 00:04:27,118
In 4000 samples, the noise drops by 28 decibels.

105
00:04:26,979 --> 00:04:29,049
Nobody told the filter the answer.

106
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:32,375
It found 0.8 and -0.5 on its own.

107
00:04:33,234 --> 00:04:35,906
This is the mathematics inside echo cancellation

108
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:39,009
on phone calls, noise-cancelling headphones, modems,

109
00:04:39,015 --> 00:04:40,236
and medical monitors.

110
00:04:41,692 --> 00:04:42,902
Now scale it up.

111
00:04:43,082 --> 00:04:45,765
A neural network's error landscape is not a smooth

112
00:04:45,626 --> 00:04:46,189
bowl.

113
00:04:46,396 --> 00:04:49,281
It is rugged, and it has not two dimensions, but

114
00:04:49,142 --> 00:04:51,212
billions: one for every weight.

115
00:04:52,078 --> 00:04:53,612
The rule does not change.

116
00:04:53,784 --> 00:04:56,446
Backpropagation computes the gradient for every

117
00:04:56,307 --> 00:04:57,361
weight at once.

118
00:04:57,553 --> 00:05:00,404
Small random batches of data make each step cheap,

119
00:05:00,510 --> 00:05:02,401
exactly as Widrow and Hoff did.

120
00:05:03,275 --> 00:05:05,892
Refinements such as momentum and Adam smooth the

121
00:05:05,753 --> 00:05:08,336
path, and the learning rate is warmed up, then

122
00:05:08,197 --> 00:05:09,262
slowly decayed.

123
00:05:09,435 --> 00:05:12,051
But underneath, it is still Cauchy's idea.

124
00:05:13,514 --> 00:05:15,628
So, what is gradient descent?

125
00:05:15,808 --> 00:05:18,558
An algorithm that reduces an error by repeatedly

126
00:05:18,419 --> 00:05:20,076
stepping against its gradient.

127
00:05:20,911 --> 00:05:22,244
When was it invented?

128
00:05:22,417 --> 00:05:24,777
In eighteen forty-seven, by Cauchy.

129
00:05:24,969 --> 00:05:26,503
How is it used in A.I.?

130
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:29,159
It adjusts the weights of a model, a little after

131
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:31,648
every batch of data, until the predictions stop

132
00:05:31,509 --> 00:05:32,250
improving.

133
00:05:33,109 --> 00:05:35,848
Next: how does a network find the gradient for

134
00:05:35,709 --> 00:05:37,209
a billion weights at once?

135
00:05:37,389 --> 00:05:38,968
That is backpropagation.
